# Games, played far enough in to show a board rather than a
# title screen.

shot    advint
for     advint advcom
cap     ADVSYS, David Betz's adventure system.  It plays a world COMPILED by
cap     advcom, and no compiled world ships -- the sample .adv source does,
cap     in GAMES/ADVSYS, and building it needs a shell with a real chd
cap     because advcom opens its include relative to the DATA directory.
cap     GAMES/ADVSYS/README has the three lines.  This is what asking for
cap     one anyway looks like.
run     advint /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/objects
wait    8

shot    backgammon
cap     Backgammon against the computer: the board drawn in characters and
cap     the dice rolled for you.
run     backgammon
wait    4
send    N\r
wait    4
send    N\r
wait    6

shot    bincheckr
cap     bincheckr reads GNU Chess's opening book -- and reports booksize 0
cap     for a book of 145 KB, then aborts.  DOC/STATUS credited it as
cap     working given a file; this is what it actually does.
run     bincheckr /dd/GAMES/gnuchess.book
wait    8
kill

shot    blackjak
cap     Las Vegas blackjack, SNOBOL4, with a hand dealt.
run     blackjak
wait    4
send    no\r
wait    6

shot    checkgame
cap     game replays a saved chess game move by move.
run     checkgame /dd/GAMES/gnuchess.book 1 3
wait    8

shot    convert
for     convert vtxtcn mkindex advcom
cap     The data builders: `convert' and `vtxtcn' make WORLD's tables, and
cap     advcom compiles an ADVSYS world.  They write files and print
cap     little, which is why the sweep could never credit them.
run     advcom
wait    8
run     convert
wait    8

shot    fuddle
cap     fuddle, a chess variant, one move in.
run     fuddle
wait    6
send    y\r
wait    6
send    e2e4\r
wait    10

shot    gnuchess
for     gnuchess gnuchessn gnuchessr
cap     THIS build of GNU Chess will not start on this terminal: `'vt100':
cap     Unknown terminal type'.  It wants a terminfo database, and what the
cap     disk has is a termcap.  `nchess' and `gnuchessc' are the builds that
cap     play -- both are here, and both have their own screen.
run     gnuchess
wait    10

shot    gnuchess-builds
for     gnuchessn gnuchessr infocom.tcap
cap     Two more GNU Chess builds -- ncurses and raw -- kept because they
cap     do not behave the same on every terminal.
run     gnuchessr
wait    8
send    e2e4\r
wait    10

shot    gnuchessc
cap     GNU Chess 4.0 with the curses display, after 1. e4.
run     gnuchessc
wait    6
send    e2e4\r
wait    10

shot    hotel
cap     hotel, a two-player board game played by coordinates -- the board
cap     as it stands at the start.
run     hotel
wait    4
send    N\r
wait    4

shot    infocom
cap     The Infocom Z-machine interpreter playing a real story file --
cap     GAMES/INFORM/dejavu.z3, compiled with Inform and shipped here.  It
cap     opens with a screenful of brackets, which is its status line meeting
cap     a terminal it cannot address; the prose underneath is the game.
run     infocom /dd/GAMES/INFORM/dejavu.z3
wait    8
send    \r
wait    4
send    look\r
wait    6
send    \r
wait    4
send    inventory\r
wait    6

shot    mkdict
for     mkdict mkindex
cap     mkdict and mkindex build Boggle's dictionary and its index.  The
cap     built dictionary is already in GAMES/BOG -- 80 KB of it -- so here
cap     they are reading the word list and writing it again, and `wc' shows
cap     what came out.
run     head -n 400 /dd/GAMES/BOG/dict > /dd/tmp/words
wait    8
run     mkdict < /dd/tmp/words > /dd/tmp/newdict
wait    10
run     wc /dd/tmp/words /dd/tmp/newdict
wait    8

shot    nchess
cap     GNU Chess 4.0 with its plain display -- the board as characters,
cap     which is how it was played over a serial line.
run     nchess
wait    6
send    e2e4\r
wait    8

shot    poker
cap     Cold-hand poker dealing a hand.  It wants the word `no', not `N',
cap     and says so in its own voice.
run     poker
wait    4
send    no\r
wait    6

shot    postprint
cap     postprint writes a chess position as PostScript for printing.  It
cap     gets one line out -- the page setup -- and stops there.
run     postprint | head -n 20
wait    8

shot    queens
cap     The N-queens problem, solved and drawn -- an IOCCC entry that reads
cap     the board size on standard input.  Six here, and it prints every
cap     solution it finds.
run     echo 6 | queens | head -n 21
wait    8

shot    stone
cap     The stones game -- take stones from the pile, do not take the last.
run     stone
wait    4
send    no\r
wait    5

shot    tttt
cap     Tic-tac-toe on a four-by-four board, mid-game.
run     tttt
wait    4
send    b1\r
wait    3
send    c2\r
wait    4
